  "top_concentration_uM": 10,
  "dilution_factor": 3,
  "n_points": 10,
  "replicates": 3,
  "controls": [
    "0 % DMSO vehicle (max activity)",
    "0 % DMSO + no enzyme (background)",
    "positive control: staurosporine (reference inhibitor)"
  ],
  "rationale": "IRAK1 is a serine/threonine kinase; the ADP‑Glo luminescent assay provides a direct measurement of catalytic activity and is widely used for kinase IC50 determinations. The chosen ATP concentration (10 µM) is below the reported Km for many kinases, increasing sensitivity to competitive inhibitors while matching conditions reported for IRAK1 assays in the retained examples (CHEMBL256570, CHEMBL256961). A 10‑point, 3‑fold dilution from 10 µM yields a concentration range of 10 µM → ~0.02 µM, covering the nanomolar‑micromolar window typical for kinase inhibitors. Replicates (n=3) and standard controls enable assessment of assay robustness and allow normalization of compound‑specific luminescence changes.",
  "citations": [
    "CHEMBL256570",
    "CHEMBL256961"
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "No direct biochemical IC50 for REBASTINIB on IRAK1 is provided in the source data; the experiment will generate a novel measurement.",
    "The ATP concentration is fixed at 10 µM; if IRAK1 has a much lower Km, the assay may underestimate potency of ATP‑competitive inhibitors.",
    "Compound solubility above 10 µM is assumed; if REBASTINIB precipitates, the effective concentration will be lower than nominal.",
    "Luminescence readout can be affected by compound fluorescence or quenching; a counter‑screen (e.g., ADP‑Glo without enzyme) is recommended but not included in the core protocol."
  ]
